{
  "reason": "The current taste-aware SASRec achieves macro validation HR@10 of 0.520 versus the baseline's 0.505, but its gains concentrate in CDs_and_Vinyl while the other three categories decline. This motivates testing whether uniform history pooling dilutes relevant intent, without treating that explanation as established. Historical gated-attention results support contextual-query retrieval inside the existing network, while failed standalone item-attention and recency-weighted-loss experiments favor retaining the transformer, sampled-softmax objective, and uniform supervision weights. Modify model_design for learned causal taste pooling and training_evaluation for faithful checkpoint reconstruction and controlled comparison.",
  "module": [
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": null,
    "model_design": "Replace only raw.cumsum(1) / count inside SASRec.features(self, seq) with single-head attention using learned projections of transformer states x as queries and raw item embeddings as keys and values, computing scaled dot-product logits plus a jointly learned relative-distance bias indexed by query position minus key position. Allow each valid query to attend to nonpadding items at positions j <= i, including its current input item because that item precedes the supervised target; initialize the distance bias to zero so training learns recency rather than imposing the fixed decay used in the unsuccessful loss-weighting experiment. Apply a numerically safe masked softmax that returns zero context for fully masked padding queries, pass the pooled context through the existing self.taste projection, and retain self.gate, its existing -1 bias initialization, self.output_norm, normalized scoring, learned scale, and jointly trained item_bias. Preserve SASRec's constructor and features, score_features, predict, history_negative_mask, and training_loss signatures and existing variable names, adding attention parameters within the same jointly trained network. Keep the transformer dimensions, blocks, heads, dropout, 192 shared uniform negatives, complete training-history exclusion, positive-collision masking, and uniform per-user-normalized transition weights unchanged to isolate learned retrieval.",
    "training_evaluation": "Update write_predict_script() so working/predict.py reconstructs the modified SASRec through the existing train.py import and saved config, strictly loads every attention and relative-bias parameter, and preserves predict(user_id, history, candidates, *, dataset_name=None), all four routes, left padding, truncation, and the identical model.predict scoring path. Preserve train_category(name, device), validation_cases, evaluate, checkpoint filenames, and exactly one freshly trained network with one validation-HR@10-selected checkpoint per category; report their macro-average HR@10 as the experiment objective without checkpoint averaging. Keep the supplied preprocessing and splits, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, fixed local validation RNG seed 42, 200 validation users per category, 99 unique negatives, random-window sampler, AdamW settings, clipping, batch size, validation cadence, 40-epoch stopping interval, and environment-controlled 200-epoch default unchanged. Before training, use small deterministic checks without advancing training RNG state to verify causal and padding masks, finite empty-history outputs, distance indexing, and checkpoint/predictor score parity. Compare the pinned host macro validation result against 0.520 and inspect all four category results without assuming improvement, retaining DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local WORK artifacts, single-GPU execution, and the shared search budget, with no hidden-test access, external data, other-trial checkpoints, independent auxiliary scorers, or evaluator changes."
  }
}
